On Mon, 21 Aug 2006, John H. wrote:
> Topi Linkala wrote:
>> Up and down are not shift-period and shift-comma
>> but < and >. In my finnish keyboard they are on same key < is unshifted
>> and > is shifted.
>
> Ah, interesting.  There's always something else to learn!  I'll
> remember in the future that international keyboards are likely to be
> different for those kinds of commands.

   They're /not/ different --- they're exactly the same!  '>' (which looks
like "go on") means "go down", and '<' (the opposite direction) means
"go up". Period and comma have absolutely nothing to do with it.

   Someone recently posted that "Shift-2" turned off autopickup, and
was also corrected; '@' (which looks like an "a", the first letter of
"autopickup") toggles autopickup, and in this context has nothing to do
with the number 2.

   I'm not surprised that so many posters[1] assumed that their keyboard
layout was the only possible keyboard layout, but I am extremely surprised
that they would go out of their way to describe characters in terms of
"Shift-plus-unrelated-character". Do you guys also refer to ampersands
as "shift-sevens" and dollar signs as "shift-fours"?

